## 1. JELLYFIN — vendor's own posture on unauthenticated media endpoints

### 1a. The canonical issue: #1501 "Video streams completely unauthenticated" (2019-07-01)
https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/issues/1501 — opened 2019-07-01, closed 2021-04-09 (labels: bug, confirmed, security). Reporter, on Jellyfin 10.3.5:

> "It was somewhat shocking to discover that Emby/Jellyfin allows streaming of a video (provided you have the ID) without any need for authentication."
> Repro: "1. Find a video ID 2. Access `/Videos/{id}/stream.{container}` with any player without any authentication"

Maintainer/contributor comments in thread:
- @ddurdle (2019-07-12): "This is an issue that was inherited from emby. The streams will take a session parameter, but it is not required. So it is possible for users to stream without authorization. I brought up the issue with emby back in emby 3.5.2."
- @angauber (2020-06-10): also affects images — `curl http://server_url/Items/hash_id/Images/Primary/ --output res.jpg` works; audio (`/Audio/hash_id/universal`) *does* return "Access token is required."
- Closed by @cvium (2021-04-09) as "Consolidating security issues/questions in #5415" — **closed as `completed`, not `not planned`**, which @pktiuk challenged in 2024 ("Closing this issue may be misleading for users and developers"); @cvium: "The consolidation was on purpose."

**NOT FIXED as of 2026.** Successor issue #13984 (split out 2025-04-23) is **OPEN**: https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/issues/13984

MEASURED reproduction on that issue, @AndreasKralj (2026-06-29):
> "I confirmed that this is repeatable on a public Cloudflare-exposed instance: a logged-in session's `stream.mp4` URL plays in an incognito browser with no further authentication… The token embedded in the URL is the user's long-lived session api_key (see #10808); therefore, a copied URL is reusable indefinitely."

Vendor rationale claim (community, contested): tokens must ride in the URL because Chromecast/Roku "can't send auth headers." @Croydon (2026-07-07) refuted it with links to Chromecast `NetworkRequestInfo#headers` and Roku `ifHttpAgent.AddHeader` docs.

### 1b. **THE VENDOR STATEMENT YOU ASKED FOR** — issue #5415
https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/issues/5415 — "Collection of *potential* security issues in Jellyfin," opened 2021-03-08 by @GermanCoding, closed 2025-04-23 (split into per-endpoint issues, most still OPEN).

Jellyfin project lead **@joshuaboniface**, 2021-04-06 — this is Jellyfin's own public posture statement:
> "Security is indeed important, and I don't want to give the impression that we don't care about security. The issue is that a lot of breaking changes, especially if they affect well-used functionality, is not a good route to take in the name of security… **Ultimately we expect users to have some ideas of the risks they take exposing instances to the Internet. No software is totally secure, and we're nowhere near the top - we admit that freely. A lot of this stuff is inherited from Emby's poor coding and security practices**, and while we would like to rewrite everything to fix it all, volunteer time is limited."

Same thread, @joshuaboniface 2021-03-08: "We are aware of quite a few, but for reasons of backwards compatibility they've never been fixed."
@anthonylavado 2021-03-10: "we have (an estimated) 35k+ installs, which is probably on the low side… There's probably about a dozen third party clients to notify when we do make changes."

**The #5415 inventory of unauthenticated / under-authenticated surfaces** (verbatim highlights):
- `VideosController`, `HlsSegmentController`, `LiveTvController` streams — "HlsSegmentController has a comment saying 'Can't require authentication just yet due to seeing some requests come from Chrome without full query string'"
- `SubtitleController` — "Allows anyone who is able to retrieve the item id to download subtitles, even if not logged in"
- **All** `AudioController` endpoints — "Has a comment saying 'TODO: In order to authenticate this in the future, Dlna playback will require updating'"
- All raw image endpoints (`ImageByNameController`, `ImageController`, `RemoteImageController`) — "allows probing on whether a specific image exists on the server by guessing item id's (which can be done without too much trouble, **as item ids are based on filepath and filename information**)"
- `PluginsController` — user-priv only: "**This includes the possibility of retrieving LDAP access credentials without admin privileges**"
- `GetUser()` — "fully unauthenticated"
- `GetSystemInfo()` — unauthenticated
- **"Authentication and user ids are decoupled"** — "affects at least 27 controllers": "If you modify your local storage to use an admin's user id, the UI starts showing the admin dashboard and the admin user's data (continue watching, favorites etc)… **You can still mess with the others user data — you can for example list or modify their favorites, recently watched, their progress on movies and series, see what they watched** and so on, but you do not gain admin access."

**Currently OPEN split issues** (all created 2025-04-23, all still open as of this search):
| # | Title |
|---|---|
| #13983 | GetAttachment() (VideoAttachmentsController) endpoint is unauthenticated |
| #13984 | Video streams unauthenticated (VideosController, HlsSegmentController, LiveTVController) |
| #13985 | Subtitle endpoints unauthenticated |
| #13986 | All endpoints in AudioController are unauthenticated |
| #13987 | Most endpoints in DashboardController are unauthenticated |
| #13988 | All (raw) image endpoints … are unauthenticated |
| #13991 | GetUser() endpoint (UserController) is fully unauthenticated |
| #13992 | Granular apikey permissions |

### 1c. Jellyfin dismissal of a 2026 re-report
#17057 (2026-06-09, Jellyfin 10.11.11) — user copies a `stream.mp4` URL from devtools, plays it in a fresh browser with no login. Closed **as invalid in 1h49m** by @nielsvanvelzen: "Aside from the video endpoints not using any authorization the screenshot clearly shows your access token as part of the URL so even if authorization was required that specific link would still work. Closing as invalid." https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/issues/17057

### 1d. **Jellyfin's security reporting channel was dead** (negative finding, MEASURED)
#17580, 2026-08-08: `security@jellyfin.org` bounced with `451 4.7.5 DKIM key retrieval failed`. Fallback `niels@jellyfin.org` **also bounced**; the team routed reports to a personal address (`joshua@boniface.me`) while fixing DKIM. https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/issues/17580

### 1e. Documentation posture — NOT the phrasing you expected
https://jellyfin.org/docs/general/post-install/networking/ — verbatim:
> "forwarding its Ports directly to the internet (**not recommended!**)"
> "Opening a port directly to the Internet is therefore insecure and not recommended."
> "While Jellyfin supports HTTPS, it is strongly recommended to handle HTTPS termination separately on a reverse proxy."
> "Self-signed certificates pose security and compatibility issues and are strongly discouraged."

**NOT REACHED / NEGATIVE:** I found **no** Jellyfin documentation page containing the sentence "Jellyfin is not designed to be exposed to the internet" or equivalent explicit disclaimer. The closest official statement is the maintainer comment in 1b above ("we expect users to have some ideas of the risks they take exposing instances to the Internet"). `jellyfin.org/posts/` lists no security-release posts on the front page; `jellyfin.org/posts/jellyfin-release-10.11.7/` → 404. `jellyfin.org/docs/general/post-install/networking/advanced/exposing/` → 404.

### 1f. Measured internet-exposure scan (bonus, from #11763)
@hlinden, 2024-05-21, on `/System/Info/Public` leaking `LocalAddress`:
> "With my quickly collected dataset of **10000 random, online-ish Jellyfin servers, 6.3% published an IPv6 address with a public prefix** as of right now."
Jellyfin dev @gnattu had asserted "For 99.99% of time this address is a LAN only address"; the scan refuted it. https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/issues/11763

