### CVE-2020-5741 detail (Tenable TRA-2020-32)
https://www.tenable.com/security/research/tra-2020-32
- Researcher: **Chris Lyne**, Tenable
- Chain: a plugin's Dict file "the contents are **unpickled without validation**"; attacker delivers a malicious Dict file **via the Camera Upload feature**, then modifies the local app-data path so it is unpickled on plugin restart → arbitrary Python execution.
- Precondition per Tenable: "**Authenticated with admin privileges**" (NVD encodes this as `PR:H`). Affected: PMS prior to **1.19.3**.
- Timeline: **2020-03-31** reported → **2020-05-07** Plex ships 1.19.3 and advisory published.
- Exploit published: packetstorm "Plex Unpickle Dict Windows Remote Code Execution" http://packetstormsecurity.com/files/158470/
- **CISA KEV**: https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog?field_cve=CVE-2020-5741

### The LastPass link — vendor claim vs. attribution
**LastPass's own wording** (https://blog.lastpass.com/posts/2023/03/security-incident-update-recommended-actions), verbatim, Incident 2:
> "The threat actor targeted a senior DevOps engineer by exploiting **vulnerable third-party software**. The threat actor leveraged the vulnerability to deliver malware, bypass existing controls, and ultimately gain unauthorized access to cloud backups."

**LastPass never names Plex in that disclosure.** Threat-actor activity ended by **October 26, 2022**; the incident began **August 2022**.

**Plex is named only secondhand.** The Record (2023-03-01, https://therecord.media/plex-unaware-of-vulnerabilities-lastpass-hacked/): "Ars Technica **reported that the third-party media software package was Plex, citing a person briefed by LastPass**." Plex's own statement: *"We are not aware of any unpatched vulnerabilities, and as always, we invite people to disclose issues to us."*

**Dates (MEASURED):** Android Police (https://www.androidpolice.com/lastpass-breach-plex-update/) — the CVE is **CVE-2020-5741**; the fix shipped in **1.19.3 on May 7, 2020**, the same day of disclosure; a **LastPass spokesperson** said "**the version that addressed this exploit was roughly 75 versions ago**." → **≈27 months (May 2020 → Aug 2022) between patch and compromise**, on a home machine.

**NOT REACHED:** arstechnica.com and pcmag.com both refuse this egress ("Claude Code is unable to fetch"). The Ars primary attribution is confirmed only via The Record's quotation of it. I could **not** source the exact Plex version the engineer ran.

### Plex household profiles — the vendor's own disclaimer (**the key quote**)
https://support.plex.tv/articles/203815766-what-is-plex-home/ — verbatim:
> "Members of a Plex Home can have a PIN code set on their account… For instance, parents may wish to put a PIN on their account so that their children can't freely switch to the parent account. **Note: Remember that setting a PIN on an account is not a replacement for good account security. A PIN should not be considered true security and is provided as a convenience to help control access when young children are involved.**"

Structural point, same page + https://support.plex.tv/articles/203948776-managed-users/:
> "Managed Users **do not have any email address associated with their account and cannot directly sign in to any app**. (The admin or another full account that's a member of the Plex Home needs to sign in to the app and then the Managed User can be switched to.)"
> "Once in a Plex Home, **you can switch to any other member of the Home in supported apps**. The only limitation is that members protected with a PIN will need to be unlocked."

→ **MEASURED design fact:** a Plex Managed User is not an authentication principal at all. It is a UI mode entered from an already-authenticated full member's session. Limits: 15 total members (14 managed + admin) on free; one Plex Home per account.

And CVE-2025-34158 is precisely the household-boundary failure: **any authenticated non-owner user of a shared server could read `/myplex/account` and obtain the server owner's administrative access token**, then pivot via `/api/resources` to every other server the owner could reach — described by the researcher as "recursive exploitation." Timeline: reported **2025-07-01**, patched **1.42.1, 2025-08-07/08**; further token issues reported **2025-08-27 to 08-31** (which became CVE-2025-69414/15/16/17, published 2026-01-02). https://github.com/lufinkey/vulnerability-research/blob/main/CVE-2025-34158/README.md · Plex's post: https://forums.plex.tv/t/plex-media-server-security-update/928341

