## 4. NETFLIX — **YES, there is a documented statement, in Netflix's own Privacy Statement**

https://help.netflix.com/legal/privacy — Section C, "Access to Account and Profiles". Verbatim:

> "If you share or otherwise allow others to have access to your account, they may be able to see viewing information, account information in the 'Account' section of the service, and game related information such as text chat contents and saved game information. **This remains true even when you use the profiles feature**, although you can add a Profile Lock PIN to restrict streaming access on a profile (see help.netflix.com/node/114277)."

> "Profiles: Profiles allow users on a Netflix account to have a personalized Netflix experience… as well as separate watch histories. **Please note that profiles are available to everyone who uses your Netflix account, so that anyone with access to your Netflix account can navigate to and use, edit or delete profiles, and information within or associated with the profile** (although, as noted above, you can add a Profile Lock PIN to restrict streaming access on a profile). You should explain this to others with access to your account…"

This is as close to "profiles are not a security boundary" as a vendor gets: the boundary is the **account**, the profile is a personalization layer, and the PIN is scoped narrowly — Netflix's own words are "**restrict streaming access on a profile**", not restrict data access.

**Profile Lock scope** (https://help.netflix.com/en/node/114277, "How to add, edit, or remove a profile PIN"):
> "To restrict access to a specific profile on your account, you can protect it with a Profile Lock PIN. **You'll enter this PIN to open the profile or play titles downloaded from it.**"
> "Note: **Extra member accounts have 1 profile and it can't have a profile lock.**"
> "How to recover a Profile Lock PIN: If you forget your Profile Lock PIN, tap or select **Forgot PIN?** or go to netflix.com/account and follow the steps above to edit or remove your PIN."

PIN is **4 digits** (10⁴ keyspace). PIN removal on web requires the account password or an emailed/SMS code — i.e. **the PIN is subordinate to the account credential, and anyone holding the account credential can delete any profile's PIN.** Profile creation can be gated ("Require PIN to add new profiles") but only from the main/first profile.

Netflix's separate security page (https://help.netflix.com/en/node/13243, "How to keep your account secure") is entirely about passwords, phishing, and `Manage Access & Devices` — **it never mentions profiles as a protection**. Negative finding, and a consistent one.

---

## 5. OTHER HOUSEHOLD-APP PROFILE ISOLATION FAILURES

### Jellyfin — per-user library restrictions leak across clients (OPEN, unfixed)
- **#8730** "Roku 1.6 Access controls are broken. Leaking restricted search results." (2022-11-12, **still OPEN**): "The search function is allowing users without access to libraries to see the results of the restricted libraries. However, on other apps such as web browser and android, the access controls are working as expected." Partially fixed in 10.8.8; reporter confirmed 2023-01-08 "**it is not working. The new version still leaks search results for movies, and access restrictions are still circumvented**"; independently confirmed still broken on 10.8.9 by two other users. https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/issues/8730
  → **The isolation is enforced per-client, not per-server.** The same server + same restricted user leaks or doesn't depending on which app is asking.
- **#16135** "Privacy/Security Issue, Collections library leaking genre" (2026-01-29, **OPEN**, Jellyfin 10.11.6): a restricted user sees genres from libraries they were denied, via the Collections genre filter. Maintainer @dfederm could not reproduce; two independent users (@D3478, @wkearney99 2026-07-22: "If I set a user to NOT have access to a certain library, genre view allows accessing the excluded library's content") confirmed it. https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/issues/16135
- **#5415 core finding, restated:** "Authentication and user ids are decoupled… affects at least 27 controllers." Swapping the user id in localStorage yields *another household member's* continue-watching, favorites, and watch history — and lets you modify them. That is a direct, documented, still-open household-profile isolation break.
- **#10732** "User profile save button allows blank password to be set" (2023-12-15, closed). https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/issues/10732

### Emby (Jellyfin's upstream) — NVD `keywordSearch=emby`, Emby-proper:
| CVE | Published | CVSS | Summary |
|---|---|---|---|
| CVE-2020-26948 | 2020-10-10 | 9.8 CRIT | SSRF via `Items/RemoteSearch/Image` <4.5.0 |
| CVE-2021-25827 | 2023-06-28 | **9.8 CRIT** | **Login bypass by setting `X-Forwarded-For` to a local IP** <4.7.12.0 |
| CVE-2021-25828 | 2023-06-28 | 6.1 M | XSS via `/web` <4.6.0.50 |
| CVE-2021-32833 | 2021-09-09 | 8.6 H | Arbitrary file read on Windows, incl. `/Videos/Id/hls/...` — 4.6.4.0, "**may not be patched in later versions**" |
| CVE-2022-36223 | 2022-12-16 | 6.1 M | Playlist-name stored XSS → steal admin token / take over admin account |
| CVE-2023-33193 | 2023-05-30 | **9.1 CRIT** | Administrative access "by spoofing certain headers which are intended for interoperation with reverse proxy servers" |
| CVE-2023-4167 | 2023-08-05 | 6.1 M | XSS in `/web/` 4.7.13.0 |
| CVE-2024-30931 | 2024-06-25 | 6.1 M | Stored XSS → privesc via `notifications.html` 4.8.3.0 |
| CVE-2025-64325 | 2025-11-18 | 9.0 CRIT | Manipulated `X-Emby-Client` injected unsanitized into admin dashboard <4.8.1.0 |
| CVE-2025-64113 | 2025-12-09 | **9.8 CRIT** | "allow an attacker to gain **full administrative access** to an Emby Server… **Other than network access, no specific preconditions need to be fulfilled**" <4.9.1.81 |

Note the twin: **Emby CVE-2021-25827 (X-Forwarded-For → local IP → login bypass)** and **Jellyfin CVE-2025-32012 (source-IP spoof → treated as LAN → unauthenticated `/System/Restart`)** are the same class inherited into both forks. Jellyfin also carries #17397 "If X-Forwarded-For contains a list of IPs, Jellyfin reads the wrong one" (2026-07-21) and #8533 "Remote IP address filter has no effect on RFC1918 addresses (they are always allowed)" (2022-10-10).

### Kodi
**NOT REACHED / NEGATIVE.** `gh search issues "profile password bypass" --repo xbmc/xbmc` returned **zero** results. `gh search issues --owner MediaBrowser` (Emby) returned **zero** — Emby's tracker is not on GitHub in a searchable form. No sourced Kodi profile-isolation failure found; I will not assert one from memory.

### Household-adjacent (the request/discovery layer that sits in front of these servers)
- **CVE-2026-27707** (9.8 CRIT, 2026-02-27): Seerr — an unauthenticated attacker points `POST /api/v1/auth/jellyfin` at *their own* Jellyfin server and gets a session on *your* Plex-configured Seerr. Chained with **CVE-2026-27793** (`GET /api/v1/user/:id` returns full settings incl. Pushover/Pushbullet/Telegram credentials to any authenticated requester) → "**a zero-prior-access chain that leaks third-party API credentials for all users, including administrators**."
- **CVE-2026-27792** (5.4): Seerr push-subscription routes missing `isOwnProfileOrAdmin()` — "allows authenticated users to access and modify data belonging to other users." A literal missing per-profile authorization check.

---

## SUMMARY OF WHAT IS VENDOR-CLAIM vs MEASURED

| Claim | Status |
|---|---|
| Jellyfin video/audio/image/subtitle streams are unauthenticated by design, inherited from Emby | **MEASURED + VENDOR-ADMITTED** — #1501, #5415, and 2026 independent repro on a live Cloudflare-exposed instance (#13984) |
| Jellyfin will not fix it because of client backwards-compat | **VENDOR CLAIM**, on the record from the project lead (#5415, 2021-04-06) |
| Jellyfin docs say "not designed to be exposed to the internet" | **NOT REACHED** — no such sentence found; nearest is the maintainer comment + "not recommended!" in the networking doc |
| CVE-2020-5741 was the LastPass vector | **VENDOR-CONFIRMED ONLY AS "vulnerable third-party software"**; "Plex" comes from Ars Technica citing "a person briefed by LastPass," and a LastPass spokesperson's "roughly 75 versions ago" |
| Plex PINs are not security | **VENDOR'S OWN WORDS**: "A PIN should not be considered true security" |
| Netflix profiles are not a privacy/security boundary | **VENDOR'S OWN WORDS**, Privacy Statement §C: "This remains true even when you use the profiles feature" |
| Household profile isolation fails differently per client app | **MEASURED** — Jellyfin #8730 (Roku leaks, web does not), #16135 (Collections genre) |
| 6.3% of internet-reachable Jellyfin servers leak a public-prefix IPv6 (incl. SLAAC MAC) | **MEASURED**, n=10,000, 2024-05-21, #11763 — and it refuted the maintainer's "99.99%" estimate |
````

